        Structural edits — converting a block, deleting one, duplicating one — go through the editor's command stack.
        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes them and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> redoes them. Undoing a delete restores
        the whole subtree with its original ids, so a table comes back with its rows.
        While the caret is inside a block you are still typing into, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> stays with the browser
        and undoes the last characters — you never lose a sentence to a structural undo.
